# rules: swift-credential-is-a-literal
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-798.swift
# from: CWE-798 (Use of Hard-coded Credentials), CWE-259, CWE-321
#
# An iOS app ships to a device the attacker owns. An `.ipa` is a zip file, and
# `strings` over the Mach-O binary inside it prints every literal the app was
# compiled with - so a key in a `let` is a key everybody who installed the app
# already has, and rotating it means shipping a release and waiting.
#
# Almost every property this rule is about has a modifier in front of it -
# `private`, `static`, `fileprivate`, `@objc` - which is the same reason the
# Kotlin rule could not be written before xen0bit/pwrq#55: Swift collects them
# into a `modifiers` node at the head of the declaration, and a pattern that
# wrote none of them anchored its first child past it.
#
# The name is what makes the rule look, and the *value* is what makes it
# report. That order matters, and the Java, C# and Kotlin passes each learned
# it the same way: a credential-shaped name holding something that is not a
# credential is the commonest false positive there is, and there are five kinds
# of it.
#
#   "password"                  a keyword in a table of them
#   "API_KEY"                   the name of an environment variable
#   "com.example.apiKey"        the name of a defaults key
#   "$(AUTH_TOKEN)"             an Xcode build setting, which is the fix
#   "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}$"      a regular expression
#
# All five are letters and separators, and a credential is not: `hunter2`,
# `s3cr3t-admin-2019` and an API key all have digits in them, because anything
# people actually use as a secret does. So the value has to hold a digit, it
# has to not be a placeholder, and it has to not be the name of somewhere the
# value lives.
#
# Two more exclusions are worth naming. The empty string is a local database
# with no password and reporting it teaches people to skip the rule; and a URL
# is not a credential however secret the host is, which keeps
# `"https://api.example.com/v2"` out of a rule whose word list contains `auth`.
#
# A value read out of the environment or the keychain is a name rather than a
# literal, and the `^"` test is what leaves those alone - it is also what makes
# the rule quiet about the fix it is asking for.

["let $N = $V", "let $N: $T = $V", "var $N = $V", "var $N: $T = $V"] as $props
| "(?i)passw|secret|^.*api[_-]?key|token|credential|private[_-]?key|passphrase"
  as $namesASecret
| "^\"" as $isALiteral
| "[0-9]" as $hasADigit
| "\\$\\(|\\$\\{|%[@sd]|^\"\\s*\"$" as $isAPlaceholder
| "^\"[a-z]+://" as $isAUrl
| "[\\[\\]]|\\(\\?|\\\\\\\\|\\.\\*|\\.\\+|\\{[0-9]+,|\\^|\\$\"?$" as $isARegex
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $props) as $all
| ( $all | of($props)
    | where_capture("N"; $namesASecret)
    | where_capture("V"; $isALiteral)
    | where_capture("V"; $hasADigit)
    | where_capture_not("V"; $isAPlaceholder)
    | where_capture_not("V"; $isAUrl)
    | where_capture_not("V"; $isARegex)
    | focus("V") )
| finding("swift-credential-is-a-literal";
    "$N is compiled into the app, so everyone who installed it has this value "
    + "and changing it means shipping a release")
| report
